"""Curated case studies, not a live vulnerability feed or exhaustive incident list."""
REVIEWED = '2026-10-30'
SCENARIOS = []
def case(slug, title, category, kind, symptom, diagnosis, cause, fix, verify, prevention, question, source, lesson):
    SCENARIOS.append(dict(id=slug,title=title,category=category,kind=kind,symptom=symptom,diagnosis=diagnosis,cause=cause,fix=fix,verify=verify,prevention=prevention,question=question,source=source,lesson=lesson,reviewed=REVIEWED))

case('graphql-body','GraphQL body exhausts server memory','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2026-0415, issued 29 October 2026: unauthenticated large POST bodies can exhaust memory in affected juniper_hyper handlers.',
'Inspect Cargo.lock and cargo tree -i juniper_hyper. Identify whether graphql() or graphql_sync() handles public input and whether an upstream limit is actually enforced.',
'Affected handlers buffered the entire body before parsing without a size bound.',
'Upgrade to juniper_hyper 0.10.1 or a compatible newer fixed version and redeploy. The documented fix defaults to a 2 MiB limit with a per-request BodyLimit override.',
'In an isolated test, check requests below and above the configured bound; oversized bodies should receive 413. Observe peak memory during concurrent requests.',
'Apply both per-request limits and concurrency budgets. A bounded body alone does not bound total process memory.',
'Why is checking Content-Length alone insufficient for a streaming request?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0415.html','http')
case('build-secret','Build artifacts accidentally contain secrets','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2026-0418, issued 29 October 2026: affected Unix builds can write environment secrets into saved build output.',
'Check for libz-sys 1.0.21–1.1.30 with cc 1.6.0 or later and review the advisory’s platform and feature exceptions. Inspect artifact exposure without printing secret values.',
'A debug-printed compiler command included explicitly populated environment variables. Cargo persisted build-script stdout in target/.',
'Upgrade libz-sys to 1.1.31 or a compatible newer fixed release. Restrict or remove affected cached artifacts and rotate exposed credentials according to their exposure.',
'Rebuild with a harmless sentinel environment value in an isolated environment; check that newly produced artifacts do not contain it. Review access to old caches.',
'Build with minimal credentials and scope artifact access. Upgrading does not erase already-published secret material.',
'How is remediation different when a secret has already left the build machine?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0418.html','release')
case('ffi-server-name','TLS binding keeps a pointer after its owner frees it','Memory safety','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2026-0413, issued 27 October 2026: certain rustls-ffi clients can read freed server-name memory.',
'Check for rustls-ffi 0.12.0–0.15.6 and calls to the client connection constructors with temporary or reused server-name buffers.',
'The constructors retained a caller-owned reference although their documented contract did not require that buffer to outlive the call.',
'Upgrade to rustls-ffi 0.15.7 or a compatible newer fixed release, which copies the input. Rebuild and redeploy every affected language binding.',
'Add a regression that constructs a client from temporary name storage and then exercises TLS processing under a memory-safety test tool.',
'Document ownership on every FFI pointer and test with caller buffers that disappear immediately after calls.',
'Why does a Rust implementation not guarantee the safety of a C-facing API?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0413.html','ffi')
case('markdown-expansion','Tiny Markdown causes huge parser output','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2026-0414, issued 28 October 2026: affected pulldown-cmark configurations can expand small Markdown inputs into excessive events.',
'Check for versions 0.13.0–0.13.4 with ENABLE_WIKILINKS, including Options::all(). The option is disabled by default.',
'Empty-display wikilinks could incorrectly connect parser node chains, multiplying later output.',
'Upgrade to 0.13.5 or a compatible newer fixed release. Disabling wikilinks mitigates this specific path when upgrading is temporarily impossible.',
'Use a small bounded regression input in isolation and assert reasonable event counts and runtime. Do not run large adversarial samples against a shared service.',
'Limit output work as well as input size; optional parser features belong in threat and regression reviews.',
'Why can a request byte limit fail to prevent algorithmic denial of service?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0414.html','property-tests')
case('tls-loop','TLS handshake enters a nonterminating I/O loop','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2024-0336: certain rustls complete_io callers can fail to terminate after a handshake close notification.',
'Identify both the resolved rustls version and whether complete_io, Stream, or StreamOwned is used. The advisory explicitly excludes callers that never use complete_io.',
'An unexpected close notification during the handshake did not lead the affected I/O routine to terminate.',
'Use a fixed compatible release: 0.21.11+, 0.22.4+, or 0.23.5+ within those branches, or a supported newer release. Consult current advisory ranges before upgrading.',
'Run a regression for early peer closure with a bounded test deadline. Confirm the rebuilt deployment uses the patched dependency.',
'Monitor handshake failures and duration. A generic async timeout cannot reliably interrupt synchronous code that never yields.',
'How would you distinguish a protocol loop from an ordinary slow peer?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2024-0336.html','tokio')
case('http2-flood','HTTP/2 continuation frames drive CPU and latency','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2024-0332: affected h2 servers can spend excessive CPU processing continuation frames.',
'Use cargo tree -i h2 and correlate CPU saturation with HTTP/2 connections and tail latency. Check for multiple resolved versions.',
'An ongoing stream of CONTINUATION frames could consume processing indefinitely; task budgeting reduced but did not eliminate service degradation.',
'Upgrade to a fixed compatible h2 release: 0.3.26 in the 0.3 branch, 0.4.4 or newer where compatible. Rebuild the application and its container image.',
'Run controlled protocol regression and mixed-traffic load tests. Verify legitimate request latency stays within your budget.',
'Combine patched protocol handling with connection limits and traffic monitoring. Rate limiting alone is not the patch.',
'Why can a server remain responsive while still suffering a denial-of-service vulnerability?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2024-0332.html','release')
case('http2-queue','Protocol resets accumulate without a bound','Security','Documented advisory',
'RUSTSEC-2024-0003: affected h2 endpoints can consume memory and CPU while queuing protocol resets.',
'Inspect h2 versions and observe memory growth with connections whose outgoing flow cannot drain. Reproduce only in a controlled environment.',
'Invalid incoming frames generated reset responses; a peer could prevent those responses from draining while the queue kept growing.',
'Upgrade to a compatible fixed h2 release, at least 0.3.24 in the 0.3 branch or 0.4.2 in the 0.4 branch. Prefer versions also addressing later advisories.',
'Check that the connection closes after bounded internal error handling, then rerun normal HTTP/2 interoperability tests.',
'Budget internal protocol queues as well as application queues.',
'Why is backpressure required inside a protocol implementation?',
'https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2024-0003.html','channels')

case('lock-await','Requests stall while a task awaits with a lock','Async & concurrency','Practice scenario',
'A cache endpoint handles light traffic but stalls when two requests overlap.',
'Trace lock acquisition and hold durations. Inspect guards that remain live across await points; reproduce on a small executor with overlapping requests.',
'A task holds a synchronous mutex while suspended, and another task blocks the executor trying to acquire it.',
'Copy the small response or update state in a short non-async method, let the guard leave scope, then await. Use an async mutex only when the protected operation must span awaits.',
'Run concurrent slow and fast requests and assert both complete within bounded deadlines.',
'Review lock scope at every await and measure contention; changing lock type alone may preserve serialization.',
'Why can avoiding a Send error by using local tasks still leave a deadlock?',
'https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/shared-state','async-locks')
case('executor-starved','A CPU-heavy request freezes unrelated timers','Async & concurrency','Practice scenario',
'A compression endpoint makes health checks slow even though database latency is normal.',
'Profile executor threads and compare timer lag with compression load. Look for synchronous work between await points.',
'The async function performs long computations without yielding; async syntax did not move computation off the executor.',
'Use a bounded CPU worker pool or limit spawn_blocking jobs with admission control. Keep request deadlines and an overload response.',
'Load the expensive endpoint while measuring health-check p95/p99 latency and CPU utilization.',
'Set an explicit concurrency budget; an unconstrained blocking pool can oversubscribe the machine.',
'Why does adding more async tasks not guarantee parallel throughput?',
'https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/task/fn.spawn_blocking.html','tokio')
case('queue-oom','An unbounded work queue exhausts memory','Async & concurrency','Practice scenario',
'Memory grows during traffic bursts and stays high while workers slowly catch up.',
'Measure arrivals, completions, queue depth, payload size, and tasks waiting for admission. Compare producer rate against sustained consumer capacity.',
'Accepted work accumulates faster than consumers can process it, with no queue or in-flight limit.',
'Use a bounded queue plus bounded admission. Choose and document rejection, blocking, or dropping behavior; apply deadlines to waiting callers.',
'Load above capacity and verify memory plateaus, queue limits hold, and rejected work is observable.',
'Size the queue from a memory and latency budget, not from the largest burst you hope to absorb.',
'What work should be dropped versus persisted for later processing?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/mpsc/fn.sync_channel.html','channels')
case('cancelled-write','A timeout leaves a partially completed operation','Async & concurrency','Practice scenario',
'A timed-out request is retried, but the remote system sometimes already performed its side effect.',
'Correlate one logical operation across client timeout, server commit, and retry. Identify cancellation points and spawned background tasks.',
'Dropping a future stops its local progress; it does not roll back remote work that already occurred.',
'Use an idempotency key and durable outcome record. Make partial progress explicit and reconcile uncertain remote outcomes rather than blindly repeating a non-idempotent call.',
'Inject cancellation before send, after send, after commit, and before response; assert one logical effect and a recoverable outcome.',
'Treat timeout as an unknown result when the operation may already have committed.',
'How would you design a retryable payment operation without promising impossible rollback?',
'https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/select','database')
case('shutdown-loss','Deployment drops accepted background jobs','Operations','Practice scenario',
'Users receive an accepted response, but some jobs disappear during rolling restarts.',
'Trace accepted job IDs through storage, worker ownership, shutdown notification, and final completion.',
'In-memory tasks were detached or the process exited before workers drained. An HTTP acknowledgment was mistaken for durable acceptance.',
'Persist accepted jobs, stop new admission, notify workers, await completion to a deadline, and leave unfinished durable jobs eligible for safe retry.',
'Restart with jobs queued, running, and committing. Check that each reaches a recorded terminal state or a retryable state.',
'Include shutdown drills in release tests and monitor unfinished work at process exit.',
'What guarantee can your accepted response honestly make?',
'https://tokio.rs/tokio/topics/shutdown','shutdown')
case('unicode-panic','Truncating a name panics on Unicode','Correctness','Practice scenario',
'ASCII profile names work, but some emoji or accented names crash preview generation.',
'Find direct string byte slicing and test a boundary inside a multibyte UTF-8 character.',
'The code treats a byte offset as a valid UTF-8 boundary or as a displayed-character count.',
'Use get for fallible byte ranges, char_indices for scalar boundaries, or a grapheme-aware library when the UI contract is visible characters.',
'Test empty strings, emoji, combining marks, and mixed scripts. Check both validity and the intended display-length rule.',
'Name offset units explicitly in APIs and distinguish byte budgets from display truncation.',
'Why can replacing byte slicing with chars().take() still split a visible character?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/string/struct.String.html','strings')
case('overflow-release','A numeric limit behaves differently in release','Correctness','Practice scenario',
'A large input panics in development but produces an unexpected small value in a release build.',
'Reproduce with the same integer type, build profile, and boundary inputs. Inspect casts and unchecked arithmetic.',
'Overflow behavior depends on the operation and overflow-check configuration; the application had no explicit overflow policy.',
'Use checked arithmetic for rejected inputs, saturating arithmetic for a documented clamp, or wrapping arithmetic only where modular behavior is intended.',
'Run boundary cases in debug and release and assert the same domain-level result.',
'Encode units and ranges with validated types; review conversions from wider to narrower integers.',
'Why is saturating arithmetic not a universal fix for financial totals?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.u32.html','types')
case('rc-leak','Memory-safe objects never get freed','Memory safety','Practice scenario',
'Repeatedly opening and closing a tree-shaped UI steadily increases retained memory.',
'Inspect strong reference counts and ownership graphs after closing a view. Separate allocator retention from genuinely live allocations.',
'Parent and child hold strong Rc references to each other, creating a cycle that never reaches a zero strong count.',
'Choose one ownership direction and use Weak for back-references. Handle upgrade returning None when an owner has disappeared.',
'Keep a weak observer, drop the root and external owners, and verify upgrade fails. Repeat under a heap profiler.',
'Draw ownership graphs during design; shared access does not always require shared ownership.',
'How does a leak differ from a use-after-free in Rust safety guarantees?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch15-06-reference-cycles.html','rc-refcell')
case('sqlite-busy','Database writes fail during concurrent requests','Data & storage','Practice scenario',
'SQLite-backed requests intermittently return busy errors when several users write at once.',
'Measure transaction durations and connection usage. Find transactions held open across network calls or user interaction.',
'SQLite permits one simultaneous writer; long write transactions block competing writers even with multiple connections.',
'Shorten write transactions, configure a bounded busy timeout, and retry only safe operations with bounded backoff. Evaluate WAL for reader/writer coexistence; it does not allow multiple simultaneous writers.',
'Run concurrent writes with injected delays and verify bounded latency, correct final data, and visible retry exhaustion.',
'Size the architecture for write load and move to a different database when the workload requires it.',
'Why can simply increasing the connection pool make contention worse?',
'https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html','database')
case('lost-update','Concurrent inventory updates overwrite each other','Data & storage','Practice scenario',
'Two successful reservations leave stock inconsistent with the accepted quantity.',
'Record transaction boundaries and reproduce two callers reading the same stock before either writes.',
'Read-modify-write logic crosses transaction boundaries or lacks a database-enforced concurrency condition.',
'Use a conditional atomic update with stock >= quantity and check rows affected. Group related writes in a transaction and persist duplicate-request protection.',
'Synchronize test callers at the read/write boundary and assert accepted quantity never exceeds available stock.',
'Treat application mutexes as process-local only; database constraints must protect multiple processes.',
'How would this design behave with two service instances?',
'https://www.sqlite.org/lang_update.html','database')
case('flaky-order','Snapshot tests pass locally and fail in CI','Correctness','Practice scenario',
'A report contains the same data but its row order changes between runs.',
'Compare rows as a multiset and inspect iteration over HashMap or HashSet. Confirm whether ordering is a promised output property.',
'The implementation relied on unspecified hash iteration order.',
'Sort at the output boundary or use an ordered collection when order is part of the contract. If order is irrelevant, assert content without imposing it.',
'Repeat with different insertion orders and fresh processes. Test tie-breaking explicitly.',
'Document ordering in public APIs and avoid normalizing tests in ways that conceal a real contract.',
'When is sorting the test output a bug rather than a fix?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html','maps')
case('poisoned-lock','A worker panic leaves a poisoned mutex','Async & concurrency','Practice scenario',
'After one worker panics, later requests panic while unwrapping a lock result.',
'Find the original panic and the protected data mutations preceding it. Later PoisonErrors are symptoms, not the first failure.',
'A panic occurred while holding the mutex, so the data might no longer satisfy its invariants.',
'Fix the initiating bug. Decide whether to fail closed, rebuild protected state, or recover after checking invariants; do not blindly ignore poisoning.',
'Inject a panic between related state changes and verify the selected recovery policy preserves consistency.',
'Minimize fallible work inside critical sections and model multi-field updates transactionally.',
'Why is recovering a guard with into_inner not itself evidence that the data is valid?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html','arc-mutex')
case('duplicate-dependency','A patched crate is still present alongside an old copy','Operations','Practice scenario',
'A dependency update was merged, but an audit still reports the vulnerable package.',
'Inspect Cargo.lock, cargo tree -d, and reverse dependencies for the exact vulnerable version. Confirm the audit checks the intended workspace.',
'Different version constraints resolved multiple copies, and only one dependency path was updated.',
'Upgrade or replace the parent dependency constraining the old version, verify the full lockfile, and rebuild the deployed artifact.',
'Audit the resolved graph and inspect all reverse paths. Confirm deployed image identity matches the rebuilt release.',
'Review lockfile diffs and retain a software inventory for each release.',
'Why can changing a direct dependency fail to remove a vulnerable transitive version?',
'https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo-tree.html','cargo-deps')

